WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.200
"Are the Pollutant Levels in Fish
High Enough to Be Harmful?"

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:09.840
As I noted in my last video
on the subject,

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:13.520
if people choose to get their
long-chain omega-3s from fish,

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:16.240
the majority of consumers
would exceed the safety limits

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:19.840
for dioxins and dioxin-like
compounds (like PCBs).

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:21.520
But is there evidence
that exposure

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:22.800
to industrial pollutants

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.360
actually has
any adverse effects on us?

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:28.240
It does appear to be the case
that the general population

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:31.520
is exposed to sufficient
persistent organic pollutants,

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:34.320
both in terms of concentration
and diversity,

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:36.160
to induce metabolic disorders,

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:39.520
raising a potential
public health concern.

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:41.920
This assumption is based
on population studies,

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:44.320
which have found higher rates
of type 2 diabetes

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:47.360
among those with elevated
body burdens of chemicals,

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:50.000
along with interventional
studies in rodents

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:52.560
that established
a cause-and-effect relationship

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:54.720
between exposure
to these chemicals

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:56.960
and the development
of metabolic disorders,

00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:59.440
including obesity
and insulin resistance.

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:02.960
If rats are fed a diet
high in farmed salmon fat,

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:05.680
for example, they gain
significantly more weight

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:08.000
compared to a diet
high in plant fat,

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:12.160
or a diet with largely
decontaminated salmon fat,

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:15.440
showing it was the contaminants
in the salmon to blame

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:17.280
for not just increasing
body fat,

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:19.040
but the worst kind of body fat:

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:20.720
deep abdominal visceral fat.

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:22.880
They also had an increase
in triglycerides

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:24.000
despite the omega-3s

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:25.360
and an increase in cholesterol,

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:27.760
along with a significant rise
in insulin levels,

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:29.760
because of a drop
in insulin sensitivity,

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:31.920
meaning they had
insulin resistance,

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:35.520
the cause of type 2 diabetes
and prediabetes.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:38.160
The researchers concluded
that exposure to POPs,

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:40.240
these persistent
organic pollutants,

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:42.560
commonly present in foods
like farmed salmon

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:44.000
can lead to insulin resistance

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:46.400
and associated
metabolic disorders.

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:50.320
Okay, but what if you eat fish
so infrequently

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:51.600
you don’t exceed

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:53.760
the U.S. Environmental
Protection Agency’s

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:54.480
safety levels?

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:57.120
Like, only having
supermarket salmon

00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:59.280
less than once every two weeks,

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:01.520
or less than once a month,

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:02.560
depending on where you buy it,

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:04.720
or less than once
every two months,

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:07.360
or once every four months?

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:09.840
The tolerable daily intakes
of these pollutants

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:12.240
are derived from things
like so-called

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:15.440
no-observed-adverse-effect
levels in animal studies,

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:17.520
where researchers
test the animal

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:19.280
most sensitive to these toxins

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:21.440
and give smaller
and smaller doses

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:22.480
until they find a dose

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:24.960
with no observable
adverse effects.

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:27.520
And then that dose
is taken and divided

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:28.880
by, like, a hundred
or a thousand

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:30.320
as a safety buffer

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:33.440
to arrive
at the tolerable human dose.

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:35.040
One of the limitations
with this approach

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:38.160
is that it tends only
to consider single chemicals,

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:39.920
one at a time.

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:42.480
What if a more
real-world experiment

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:44.400
were conducted where animals
were exposed

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:45.920
to a mixture of pollutants

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:49.840
but each at 500 times
lower than

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:51.920
the no-observed-adverse-effect
level,

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:55.360
at the quote-unquote
"tolerable" human intake?

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:57.280
Nothing should have happened,
right?

00:02:57.280 --> 00:02:59.840
Each was 500 times smaller
than the dose

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:02.000
that causes any effects.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:04.080
But when mixed together,

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:06.880
which is what actually happens
in fish and other seafood,

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:08.880
doses considered safe
for humans

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:11.520
are, in fact, not harmless
when exposure is chronic

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:14.400
and when pollutants
are administered in combination

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:18.000
in a context of what
too many Americans face.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:20.320
Because seafood represents
one of the main sources

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:21.920
of human exposure to pollutants

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:24.320
that contribute
to metabolic diseases,

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:25.920
there is a call
on policymakers

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:29.120
to urgently regulate
and diminish the concentrations

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:31.120
of pollutants in fish
and other seafood

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:33.840
to protect
the general population.

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:37.440
Until that happens,
how can we stay away from them?

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:40.240
Well, we’re exposed
to flame retardant chemicals

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:42.640
in meat, including
fish and poultry;

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:47.120
PCBs in fish,
other meat, and dairy;

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:51.280
dioxins, the same thing:
fish, other meat, and dairy.

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:54.880
We’re exposed to PFOS
(forever chemicals) in seafood,

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:56.320
other meat, eggs,
dairy products,

00:03:56.320 --> 00:04:00.160
and, in some places,
our drinking water.

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:02.160
If the highest
pollutant concentrations

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:03.920
are found in fish,

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:06.160
how are we going to get
our omega-3s?

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:08.800
The short-chain omega-3
found in walnuts and flaxseeds

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:09.760
may be sufficient,

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:11.280
since our body
can convert those

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:13.680
into the long-chain omega-3s
found in fish,

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:15.680
like EPA and DHA.

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:17.520
Pregnant
and breastfeeding women,

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:20.400
and those at greater risk
for poor conversion,

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:22.240
such as people with diabetes,
older adults,

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:23.520
and premature infants,

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:25.840
are especially most likely
to benefit

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:28.560
from algae-derived
DHA supplements,

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:32.310
which represent
a safe omega-3 source.

